  "@graph": [
    {"@id":"claim:isolation-useful","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Isolated worker state is useful for concurrent agent engineering","summary":"CAID reports substantial gains over single-agent baselines using dependency-aware delegation, isolated workspaces, structured integration, and executable verification; isolation is one mechanism in a larger bundle rather than independently isolated by that experiment.","status":"empirical-conclusion","statement":"Isolated mutable source state is a credible component of concurrent agent engineering architectures.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:caid","note":"Cited at arXiv:2603.21489v2: +25.6 absolute on PaperBench and +14.7 on Commit0 for the whole CAID bundle over single-agent baselines. Isolation is one component of that bundle; v2 Table 3 is the only within-paper comparison that isolates it."},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:storm"},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:agentroom"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"algorithm:snapshot-execution"}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:coordination-beyond-isolation","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Isolation alone is insufficient coordination","summary":"STORM reports gains over a worktree-based multi-agent baseline using explicit state management; AgentRoom's small ablations also attribute much of its benefit to coordination signals rather than CRDT merging alone.","status":"empirical-conclusion","statement":"Concurrent coding agents benefit from explicit shared coordination/state beyond merely isolated workspaces in the studied settings.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:storm"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:agentroom"},{"predicate":"challengedBy","target":"source:caid","note":"CAID v2 Table 3 compares worktree isolation against soft isolation and finds isolation wins: PaperBench 63.3 against 55.5 (below the 57.2 single-agent baseline), Commit0-Lite 59.1 against 56.1. That result weighs against this claim rather than for it."},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:semantic-claim"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"algorithm:coordination-plane"},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:lesser-2004","note":"Two decades of GPGP/TAEMS results find coordination mechanisms pay off only where task interdependence is high enough to offset their communication and commitment overhead; the claim holds in that regime, not universally."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:semantic-state-vs-code-state","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Code isolation and semantic coordination should be separate planes","summary":"Synthesis of CAID, STORM, AgentRoom, MVCC-style reasoning, and source-control constraints: workers can mutate isolated code snapshots while sharing explicit intent, claim, dependency, and interface state.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"Gordian should combine isolated code state with coordinated semantic state instead of either unconstrained shared mutation or coordination only at merge time.","relations":[{"predicate":"motivatedBy","target":"source:caid","note":"CAID v2 compares its own bundle against single-agent baselines and, in Table 3, worktree isolation against soft isolation. It runs no comparison that separates a code plane from a semantic coordination plane, so arXiv:2603.21489v2 motivates this design rather than supporting it."},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:storm"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:agentroom"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:mvcc"},{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:isolation-coordination-ablation"}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:posthoc-integration-risk","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Post-hoc merge is a material integration risk for coding agents","summary":"AgenticFlict reports textual conflicts in 27.67 percent of more than 107K successfully merge-simulated agent PRs, making conflict handling non-negligible at scale.","status":"empirical-conclusion","statement":"Agent integration conflict occurs frequently enough that Gordian should treat integration and repair as first-class rather than a negligible tail event.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:agenticflict"},{"predicate":"contrastsWith","target":"claim:textual-not-semantic-conflict","note":"Retyped from qualifiedBy: an evidence predicate must name a Source. The two claims scope each other; the qualifying evidence is now carried by source:sousa-2018 and source:khanna-2007 on the other claim."},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:integration-candidate"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"algorithm:semantic-conflict-admission"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:brun-2011","note":"Crystal's speculative merging found conflicts that persisted unnoticed for days, independently of any agent workload."},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:sarma-2012","note":"Palantir measured conflict incidence in human parallel development and is the pre-agent baseline the AgenticFlict rate must be read against."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:textual-not-semantic-conflict","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Textual conflict rate is not semantic conflict rate","summary":"A clean text merge can still violate interfaces, schemas, invariants, or behavior, while overlapping text can sometimes be reconciled safely. AgenticFlict's reported rate therefore cannot be generalized to all semantic conflicts.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"Textual merge-conflict observations are a lower-dimensional signal and not a direct estimate of total semantic incompatibility.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:agenticflict"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:semantic-convergence"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:semantic-resource"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:sousa-2018","note":"Gives a semantic criterion for a correct three-way merge, which is exactly what a clean textual merge does not establish."},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:khanna-2007","note":"Characterises what diff3 actually computes and where it produces a merge no reasonable specification sanctions."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:interface-contracts","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Interface and dependency contracts are useful pre-concurrency state","summary":"CodeTeam reports benefits from normalizing architecture into machine-checkable ownership, public-interface, and dependency constraints before concurrent implementation.","status":"empirical-conclusion","statement":"Making interfaces and dependency boundaries explicit before concurrent implementation is a credible coordination mechanism.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:codeteam"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:semantic-claim"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:verifier-manifest"},{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:semantic-conflict-prediction"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:sarma-2012"}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:harness-reliability","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Coding-agent reliability is a harness/system property","summary":"The reliability synthesis emphasizes execution state, permissions, retrieval, memory, verification, observability, recovery, and resource allocation in addition to model capability.","status":"empirical-conclusion","statement":"Gordian should place safety and correctness obligations in the execution substrate rather than depending on worker model judgment alone.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:reliable-agents"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:authority-model"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:evidence-graph"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"algorithm:candidate-admission"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:newcombe-2015","note":"Production evidence that substrate-level checking finds defects that reviewing component logic does not."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:exact-artifact-verification","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Verification should bind to exact immutable subjects","summary":"Jujutsu preserves logical change identity through rewrites while exact commit identity changes; provenance/attestation systems likewise bind claims to exact subjects and materials.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"Candidate verification must bind to exact commit/spec/input/environment/verifier identity rather than only a mutable logical work identity.","relations":[{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:jj-docs"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:in-toto"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:slsa"},{"predicate":"formalizedBy","target":"theorem:evidence-commit-mismatch"},{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:evidence-mutation"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:mokhov-2018","note":"The evidence fingerprint is a build-system key; minimality and early cutoff are the properties it is trying to buy."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:change-id-commit-id-separation","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Logical change identity and exact verification identity must be separate","summary":"A Jujutsu change ID survives rewrites while a commit ID identifies one exact concrete state, providing a natural mapping from evolving implementation identity to immutable verification subject.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"Agents may operate on logical change identity while verification applies to exact commit identity.","relations":[{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:jj-docs","note":"Change-ID stability across rewrite is documented behaviour from jj 0.23.0 onward; experiment:jj-baseline pins the exact release under test and is the contract test for this claim."},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:candidate"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:evidence"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:jj-concurrency"}],"verification":[{"method":"CLI contract test","target":"experiments/jj-baseline/","status":"planned","note":"Pinned release recorded by experiment:jj-baseline (#1)."}]},
    {"@id":"claim:jj-graph-causality","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Jujutsu graph topology should reflect causal dependency rather than finish order","summary":"Independent work can remain sibling changes; dependent work can use ancestry, and explicit integration can use multi-parent states. This avoids encoding chronology as causality.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"Gordian should not serialize independent Jujutsu changes merely because workers finish sequentially.","relations":[{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:jj-docs"},{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:jj-vs-git"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"algorithm:jj-candidate-integration"}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:crdt-not-semantic-correctness","@type":["Claim"],"name":"CRDT convergence does not imply program semantic correctness","summary":"CRDTs can guarantee convergence of replicated state under their merge semantics, while incompatible program assumptions can remain after text/state convergence.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"A converged shared source representation cannot substitute for semantic coordination and integration verification.","relations":[{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:crdt"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:agentroom"},{"predicate":"motivates","target":"concept:semantic-claim"},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:apel-2011","note":"Semistructured merge recovers part of the gap between representation convergence and semantic compatibility, so the gap is narrower than a purely textual account suggests."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"claim:deterministic-core-effectful-boundary","@type":["Claim"],"name":"Coordinator replay should be deterministic while effects are recorded externally","summary":"Durable-execution systems separate deterministic replayable orchestration from external effects. LLM calls, clocks, live network reads, and writes should become recorded activities/events rather than being repeated during replay.","status":"engineering-deduction","statement":"Gordian's core projection/reconciliation semantics should be deterministic functions of recorded history, with nondeterministic effects at explicit boundaries.","relations":[{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:temporal"},{"predicate":"formalizedBy","target":"theorem:replay-equal-history"},{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:replay-faults"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:helland-2016"}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:derived-state-reduces-staleness","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"Derived workflow state lowers the stale-coordination error rate","summary":"Readiness, blocking, activity and satisfaction derived from canonical dependencies, attempts and evidence are compared against manually maintained status fields under injected failures, candidate rewrites, delayed events and dependency changes.","status":"untested","statement":"Under the generated concurrent-Mission corpus of experiment:derived-vs-mutable-state, deriving work state from canonical facts lowers the stale-state decision rate (scheduling decisions taken against a state that the canonical facts contradict) by >= 50 percent relative versus the mutable-status baseline condition, over >= 200 seeded trials; otherwise reject.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:derived-vs-mutable-state"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:deterministic-replay"},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:temporal","note":"Temporal's durable-execution model derives state from history in production, but only for workflows whose every effect is already an Activity; Gordian's agents produce effects outside that boundary."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:semantic-claims-better","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"Semantic resource claims beat path ownership at conflict prediction","summary":"Declared read/write claims over domain resources are compared against path overlap and module ownership predictors on observed integration outcomes, with declaration and observation overhead charged against the predictor.","status":"untested","statement":"Under the AgenticFlict-derived integration corpus of experiment:semantic-conflict-prediction, semantic resource claims raise the F1 score for predicting a failing integration by >= 0.10 absolute versus the path-overlap baseline condition, over >= 500 seeded trials, after subtracting declaration and observation overhead from net completion cost; otherwise reject.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:semantic-conflict-prediction"},{"predicate":"motivatedBy","target":"source:codeteam"},{"predicate":"motivatedBy","target":"source:agenticflict"},{"predicate":"challengedBy","target":"source:owhadi-kareshk-2019","note":"Predictors trained over 744 repositories reach only modest decision quality, so the path-overlap baseline this hypothesis must beat is already weak and beating it is not evidence of a useful predictor."},{"predicate":"challengedBy","target":"source:lessenich-2018","note":"Seven developer-proposed conflict indicators were tested against 163 repositories and none predicted merge conflicts usefully; declared semantic claims are another such indicator."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:snapshot-better-than-rebase","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"Stable snapshots beat continuous active rebasing for agent workers","summary":"Workers executing against one exact frozen base are compared against workers whose active work is periodically rebased onto the accepted frontier, stratified by task duration and frontier-change rate.","status":"untested","statement":"Under the generated concurrent-workload corpus of experiment:snapshot-vs-rebase, stable snapshot execution lowers total completion-plus-repair wall time by >= 15 percent relative versus the continuous-rebase baseline condition, over >= 120 seeded trials at each of three frontier-change rates; otherwise reject.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:snapshot-vs-rebase"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:occ"},{"predicate":"motivatedBy","target":"source:caid","note":"CAID compares CAID against single-agent baselines; it reports no snapshot-versus-rebase comparison. The string 'rebase' does not occur in arXiv:2603.21489v2."},{"predicate":"challengedBy","target":"source:storm","note":"STORM reports +18.7 on Commit0-Lite for explicit state management over a Git-worktree baseline, which is the closest published result to continuous synchronisation beating stable isolation."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:atom-boundary-optimality","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"The Atom is the right global scheduling boundary","summary":"Coarse work units, Atom-local Quarks and globally scheduled fine-grained primitives are compared on exposed parallelism and coordination overhead over the same dependency graphs.","status":"untested","statement":"Under the generated Mission-graph corpus of experiment:atom-granularity, Atom-level global scheduling achieves makespan within 10 percent of the globally fine-grained baseline condition while issuing >= 40 percent fewer coordination events, over >= 200 seeded trials; otherwise reject.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:atom-granularity"},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:yang-gerasoulis-1994","note":"Dominant-sequence clustering quantifies the parallel-time cost of coarsening task granularity, bounding what an Atom-sized unit can gain over finer scheduling."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:mission-ontology-useful","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"Mission decomposition ontology lowers replan churn","summary":"Project/Mission/PlanRevision/Initiative/Atom/Quark decomposition is compared against a flat issue-and-task structure on the same engineering work, with planning overhead charged to the richer ontology.","status":"untested","statement":"Under the controlled engineering-task corpus pre-registered at experiments/mission-ontology/protocol.json (#50), the six-level decomposition lowers the replan-churn rate (Atoms whose specification is rewritten after dispatch, per completed Mission) by >= 30 percent relative versus the flat issue-and-task baseline condition, over >= 60 seeded trials; otherwise reject.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:mission-ontology"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:htn"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"foundation:pocl"},{"predicate":"challengedBy","target":"source:caid","note":"CAID v2 Table 3 obtains its gains from dependency-aware planning over a flat task list, with no six-level decomposition ontology, so the ontology's marginal contribution is unevidenced."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:jj-worth-complexity","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"A Jujutsu adapter costs fewer identity and recovery operations than a Git adapter","summary":"Equivalent Jujutsu and Git orchestration adapters are compared with Mission Graph, workers, scheduler, workload and verifier held constant.","status":"untested","statement":"Under the generated orchestration corpus pre-registered at experiments/jj-vs-git/protocol.json (#34), the Jujutsu adapter records >= 40 percent fewer combined identity errors and recovery operations per completed Mission versus the Git-worktree baseline condition, over >= 100 seeded trials; otherwise reject.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:jj-vs-git"},{"predicate":"groundedIn","target":"source:jj-docs"},{"predicate":"challengedBy","target":"source:caid","note":"CAID v2 achieves its reported gains over Git worktrees, without change-ID identity or first-class conflicts, which is the outcome this hypothesis predicts should be worse."},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:storm","note":"STORM's gains come from explicit state management on top of a Git-worktree baseline, suggesting the coordination plane rather than the source substrate carries the effect."}],"verification":[]},
    {"@id":"hypothesis:formalization-pays","@type":["Hypothesis"],"name":"Verification-guided formalization finds defects per engineering hour at a rate ordinary testing does not","summary":"Defects found uniquely by proof development and differential testing are tracked against the engineering and maintenance hours the formal layer consumes, over the safety kernel only.","status":"untested","statement":"Over >= 12 months of the defect-and-cost ledger of experiment:formal-value, proof development plus Lean/Rust differential testing find >= 1 safety-kernel defect per 40 engineering hours spent on the formal layer that the Rust test baseline condition did not find first, across >= 30 recorded defects; otherwise reject and stop expanding the formal layer.","relations":[{"predicate":"testedBy","target":"experiment:formal-value"},{"predicate":"supportedBy","target":"source:cedar-vgd-fse-2024"},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:klein-2009","note":"seL4 reports roughly 20 person-years for about 8700 lines of C, a proof-effort ratio far above anything Gordian's safety kernel can absorb; the hypothesis holds only if the kernel stays small."},{"predicate":"qualifiedBy","target":"source:hawblitzel-2015","note":"IronFleet reports a proof-to-code ratio of roughly 4:1 for verified distributed systems, which bounds the plausible cost side of this hypothesis."}],"verification":[]}
  ]
}
